# MSC category

The atlas category a Mathematics Subject Classification (MSC 2020) top-level
subject falls under, so problems from every source sit in one vocabulary of
categories, and the rank's expressibility prior reads them alike.

## To mscCategory (code: Int) -> Text:
    Let singles be [3, 5, 11, 14, 20, 22, 35, 37, 58, 60, 62, 68, 90, 91, 94].
    Let named be ["logic", "combinatorics", "number-theory", "algebraic-geometry", "group-theory", "group-theory", "pde", "dynamical-systems"].
    Let more be ["analysis", "probability", "probability", "computer-science", "computer-science", "computer-science", "computer-science"].
    Repeat for i from 1 to length of singles:
        If item i of singles is equal to code:
            Return item i of (named + more).
    If code is at least 51 and code is at most 53:
        Return "geometry".
    If code is at least 54 and code is at most 57:
        Return "topology".
    If code is at least 70 and code is at most 86:
        Return "physics".
    If [6, 8, 12, 13, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19] contains code:
        Return "algebra".
    If code is at least 26 and code is at most 49:
        Return "analysis".
    Return "miscellaneous".

## Test: MSC subjects map onto the atlas's categories

For each of these cases:

| code | category           |
|------|--------------------|
| 3    | "logic"            |
| 5    | "combinatorics"    |
| 11   | "number-theory"    |
| 13   | "algebra"          |
| 20   | "group-theory"     |
| 35   | "pde"              |
| 42   | "analysis"         |
| 52   | "geometry"         |
| 55   | "topology"         |
| 60   | "probability"      |
| 68   | "computer-science" |
| 81   | "physics"          |
| 97   | "miscellaneous"    |

Expect that mscCategory(code) is equal to category.

## Test: number theory and combinatorics are told apart

For all booleans numbers:
    Let mutable code be 5.
    If numbers:
        Set code to 11.
    Expect that (mscCategory(code) is equal to "number-theory") is equal to numbers.
